Database migrations (Alembic)
=============================

The SQLAlchemy models in ``app/models`` are the source of truth for the schema.
Alembic revisions in ``migrations/versions`` turn an empty database into that
schema and move a database from one schema version to the next.

The database URL comes from the ``DATABASE_URL`` setting, the same one the
application uses. Run every command from the ``backend`` directory.

Common commands
---------------

Create a new revision after changing a model::

    python -m alembic revision --autogenerate -m "describe the change"

Review the generated file: autogenerate cannot detect every change (for example
renamed columns or data backfills), so edit it before applying it.

Apply all pending revisions::

    python -m alembic upgrade head

Roll back the most recent revision::

    python -m alembic downgrade -1

Show the current revision of a database::

    python -m alembic current

Check that the models and the migrations agree (exits non-zero on drift)::

    python -m alembic check

Application startup
-------------------

At startup the application creates any missing tables from the models only when
``DB_AUTO_CREATE_SCHEMA`` is enabled. It is enabled by default everywhere except
``APP_ENV=production``. In production, run ``alembic upgrade head`` before starting
the application; the startup step does not change the schema there.

Existing databases
------------------

A database that was created by the application's startup ``create_all`` already
has the baseline schema. Record that without running the baseline again::

    python -m alembic stamp head

Revisions
---------

- Baseline schema, split into nine parts (``*_baseline_part_<n>_of_9.py``). Together
  they create every table, index and enum type that ``Base.metadata`` declares. The
  parts run in order, each in its own transaction, so no single transaction has to
  lock all 353 tables. Each part holds 40 tables in dependency order, so a foreign
  key only points at the same part or an earlier one. Part 9 (``16528e4bc671``) is the
  head revision, so databases created before the split are already at head.
- Downgrading a part drops its tables and indexes, and drops the enum types that the
  part created.

Testing
-------

``tests/integration/test_migrations.py`` builds a scratch database from the
revisions, checks that it matches the models exactly, and checks that
``downgrade base`` removes every table. The scratch database name must end in
``_test``.
